Dane Backbier - dbackbie - dbackbie@u.rochester.edu - Project 3 - Street Mapping

Project 3 - Street Mapping is aimed at implementing dijkstra's shortest path algorithm to find paths
between any given points in a graph and to create a GUI to visually show the path.

My code works using my URGraph, UREdge, URGraphNode, minHeap classes as well as other built-in data
structures. The main class is the StreetMap.java and is the file that is compiled and ran. It works
by first checking that enough arguments were given, then iterating through the arguments and figuring
out if whether or not it should show the graph/ directions or if the arguments are incorrect. Then it
creates a graph using using the URGraph constructor built for taking files and making a graph out of
a file. Depending on whether the user chose to show the graph or directions. If the user just wants
to show, the chosen graph will be shown without any path, just the graph. Then uses the isConnected
method to check if the start and end nodes are connected in the first place, if so it'll continue. If
both show and directions are true it'll initialized a Pair<ArrayList<URGraphNode>, ArrayList<String>>
for the path and pathIDs respectively, generated from the dijkstras method, which runs in O((V+E)log V)
time. I created a Pair class near the top of the StreetMap file so that I could return both the path
and pathIDs as I would need the pathIDs for the GUI, and decided to initialize a Pair first and
call getLeft and getRight to instead of calling getLeft and getRight on dijkstras twice as then dijkstras
would run twice, which takes too long. After assigning path to the left value and pathIDs to the right
value of the Pair, it will only continue in the path is not empty because if it does, the for loop will
cause an ErrorOutOfBoundsException. Then it'll iterate through the path list keeping track of the total
distance after each step and outputting the directions, and when it's done, the total distance. Then it
will create the GUI, it does this using an overridden paint method, which works using two passes of
the edge set, the first being drawing the lines between all edges in black, and the second iterating
through all the edges again but then only drawing the lines that are in the path ID set in red. Leading
to an O(E) runtime. But if there's no GUI being shown it'll only print out the directions as explain
previously. And if the two chosen intersections aren't connected, it will output accordingly.

The main obstacle I overcame was figuring out how to scale my original algorithm for the larger datasets
of monroe.txt and nys.txt, which did by using a min heap/ priority queue which stores the shortest distance
with at the top so I can access it in O(1) time, rather than repeatedly finding the closest node, which took
O(E) time.

